<Base
  title="How Often Should a Pool Be Cleaned in Texas?"
  description="Why weekly pool cleaning isn't enough in a Central Texas summer, and what a real schedule looks like season by season. Call (512) 764-9867."
>
  <h1>How often should your pool be cleaned in a Texas summer?</h1>

  <p>
    Once a week is the standard advice everywhere, but in a Central Texas summer, weekly is
    often the minimum, not the target — high heat accelerates chlorine burn-off and algae
    growth fast enough that a single skipped week can undo a month of steady maintenance. How
    often your pool actually needs attention depends on the season, the equipment, and how much
    debris load your yard produces.
  </p>

  <Dispatch problem="Pool falling behind on a cleaning schedule" area={SITE.serviceArea} />

  <h2>What "cleaning" needs to include, every time</h2>
  <p>
    A cleaning visit that only skims the surface is missing most of what keeps a pool actually
    healthy: testing and balancing chlorine, pH, and alkalinity; brushing walls and steps where
    algae gets a foothold that skimming never reaches; emptying skimmer and pump baskets so flow
    stays strong; and checking filter pressure so the equipment is doing its share of the work
    between visits.
  </p>

  <h2>A realistic Central Texas schedule</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <strong>Summer (June–September).</strong> Weekly is the floor. In peak heat with heavy
      pool use, twice-weekly chemical checks catch chlorine demand spikes before they turn into
      algae — especially after a pool party or a run of 100°F days.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Spring (March–May).</strong> Oak pollen season loads skimmer baskets and filters
      fast in tree-heavy neighborhoods. Weekly cleaning holds, but basket checks in between
      matter more than usual.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fall (October–November).</strong> Leaf drop brings debris load back up even as
      swimming slows down. Don't stretch the interval just because nobody's using the pool —
      decomposing leaves feed algae and throw off chemistry on their own schedule.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Winter (December–February).</strong> This is when most Central Texas pools can
      safely stretch to biweekly, since cooler water slows both chlorine loss and algae growth.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What happens when a cleaning gets skipped</h2>
  <p>
    One missed week rarely causes visible problems on its own. The risk is compounding: a missed
    cleaning means chemistry drifts, a drifted pool loses its chlorine reserve faster the next
    week, and by the third missed cycle you're often looking at a{' '}
    <a href="/guides/neglected-green-pool-recovery/">green pool recovery</a> instead of a routine
    visit. In July and August specifically, that slide can happen in under two weeks.
  </p>

  <h2>Signs your interval is too long</h2>
  <p>
    Chlorine that reads fine on Monday but is gone by Wednesday, a filter that needs backwashing
    more often than it used to, or water that looks slightly hazy right before your next
    scheduled visit are all signs the interval, not the process, needs to change. See our guide
    on <a href="/guides/cloudy-pool-water-causes/">cloudy pool water causes</a> if haziness is the
    main symptom you're chasing.
  </p>

  <h2>When to call a pro</h2>
  <p>
    Testing chemistry and skimming debris between scheduled visits is safe and worth doing
    yourself. Call in a technician when chemistry keeps drifting no matter how often you test,
    when algae shows up despite a consistent schedule (often an equipment or CYA problem, not a
    scheduling one), or when you simply can't keep the interval tight enough during peak summer
    and want a standing service instead of catching up after the fact.
  </p>

  <section class="faq">
    <h2>Common questions</h2>
    <h3>Can I stretch cleaning to every two weeks in summer to save money?</h3>
    <p>
      It's possible with a very healthy pool, strong filtration, and low bather load, but it's
      risky in Central Texas heat — a lot of pools that try this end up needing a much larger
      green-pool recovery instead of a normal visit, which usually costs more than the routine
      cleanings it was meant to save.
    </p>
    <h3>Does a saltwater system change the schedule?</h3>
    <p>
      It changes how chlorine gets generated, not how often the pool needs attention. Baskets
      still clog, salt cells still need checking, and algae still needs brushing — a salt system
      is a chlorine source, not a maintenance shortcut.
    </p>
    <h3>How do I know if my pump is running long enough to support the schedule?</h3>
    <p>
      See our guide on <a href="/guides/how-long-should-pool-pump-run-texas/">pump run times in
      Central Texas</a> — circulation and cleaning frequency work together, and a pump running
      too few hours a day will undercut even a perfect cleaning schedule.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Dispatch problem="Pool falling behind on a cleaning schedule" area={SITE.serviceArea} />
</Base>
